\title{CPMG: A Context-Conditioned Guard for Compositional and Dormant\\Memory Poisoning in LLM Agents}

\author{Shahmeer Ali \quad Mohammad Soban \quad Ali Rauf \\
  FAST National University of Computer and Emerging Sciences, Islamabad \\
  \texttt{\{23I-0119, 23I-0056, 23I-0076\}} \\
  Agentic AI (AI-4015), Instructor: Sohail Abbas}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
LLM agents store content from untrusted channels in long-term memory and reuse it later, so an attacker can plant a
record now that changes an answer or a tool call in a later session. Guards that score one record at a time cannot
see two kinds of attack: one whose harmful part sits in a different record, and one that stays inactive until a
later query supplies its trigger. We present the Contextual Provenance Memory Guard (CPMG), a DeBERTa-v3-base
cross-encoder that reads a memory record together with its source, the current query and its co-retrieved
neighbours, and outputs two scores: whether the context contains attacker-written content, and whether the current
query activates it. We build a matched evaluation from five public sources in which positives and negatives share
the scored record and differ only in a neighbour or in the query, and in which every test trigger is a real
AgentPoison trigger unseen in training. Record-only guards, including PIGuard, are at chance on these cases by
construction. CPMG detects activation by unseen real triggers with [[cpmg_full:A_L3_hard_real:auroc:mean]] AUROC
in one [[run:cpmg_full:latency_ms_bs1:.1f]]\,ms forward pass. When an LLM rewords the attack so that the trigger
is no longer quoted, a hand-written rule that had matched CPMG falls to
[[lexquote:A_L3_hard@paranq:auroc:mean]] AUROC while CPMG stays at [[cpmg_full:A_L3_hard@paranq:auroc:mean]].
We also report what did not work: the model ignores its provenance tokens, public benchmarks cannot test provenance
at all, and the model is misled by a record format it has not seen.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Agents built on large language models keep a long-term memory so that they can use earlier conversations,
documents and tool results in later tasks \citep{packer2023memgpt,park2023generative,xu2025amem}. Much of what
they store comes from channels the user does not control: email, shared documents, web pages and tool outputs. An
attacker who can write to one of these channels can place a record in memory that is retrieved in a later session
and changes what the agent does \citep{chen2024agentpoison,dong2025minja,dash2026mpbench}. Such records persist:
in one recent measurement, malicious memory survived in most cases and about half of the complete
write-then-execute chains succeeded \citep{chen2026memsecbench}.

The cheapest defence is a classifier that reads each record and flags injected instructions. PIGuard is a strong
example \citep{li2025piguard}. A classifier of this kind reads one string, and Figure~\ref{fig:levels} shows why
that is not enough. In a \emph{direct} attack (L1) the injected instruction is in the record, and a record-only
guard can find it. In a \emph{compositional} attack (L2) the instruction is in a second record; the record being
scored is clean and no score computed from it alone can be right. In a \emph{dormant} attack (L3) the instruction
waits for a trigger; the same record is harmless under one query and harmful under another, so a guard that gives
one score per record must be wrong for one of them.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\input{figs/levels}
\caption{Three levels of memory poisoning, shown on one AgentPoison record with the BadChain trigger. Orange boxes
contain attacker-written text. A record-only guard reads the first column and nothing else. In L2 that column is
clean. In L3 it is identical for the dormant and the active case.}
\label{fig:levels}
\end{figure*}

A-MemGuard handles context by comparing reasoning paths built from related memories, at the cost of several LLM
calls per check \citep{wei2026amemguard}. We ask a narrower question about cost: \emph{can a small encoder
that reads the record together with its context detect L2 and L3 attacks in a single forward pass, and what does
it actually learn when it does?}

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=1pt,topsep=2pt]
\item \textbf{CPMG}, a cross-encoder guard with two outputs, \emph{poison} and \emph{activation}, that scores a
record when it is written and again when it is retrieved (Section~\ref{sec:method}).
\item \textbf{A matched evaluation} built from five public sources, in which the context is the only signal: paired
examples share the scored record, negatives include wrong triggers and harmless repeated text, and all test triggers
are real and unseen (Section~\ref{sec:data}).
\item \textbf{Results.} Record-only guards are at chance on L2 and L3. CPMG reaches
[[cpmg_full:A_L3_hard_real:auroc:mean]] activation AUROC on unseen real triggers, and each part of its input is
necessary for exactly one capability (Section~\ref{sec:main}).
\item \textbf{A robustness test that separates the model from a rule.} A hand-written rule matches CPMG on the
original attacks and fails when an LLM rewords them; CPMG does not (Section~\ref{sec:para}).
\item \textbf{Negative findings} on provenance, on an unseen record format and on false alarms
(Sections~\ref{sec:xtemplate}--\ref{sec:false}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{Agent memory.} MemGPT, Generative Agents and A-Mem show why agents benefit from storing and
reorganising experience \citep{packer2023memgpt,park2023generative,xu2025amem}. LoCoMo and LongMemEval measure
long-term recall \citep{maharana2024locomo,wu2025longmemeval}. None of them measures resistance to poisoned
content.

\paragraph{Attacks.} AgentPoison optimises a trigger so that poisoned records are retrieved for triggered queries,
and writes a conditional instruction into those records \citep{chen2024agentpoison}. MINJA plants records through
ordinary queries \citep{dong2025minja}. Agent Security Bench includes memory poisoning among its attack families
\citep{zhang2025asb}, and MemoryGraft poisons an agent's experience store \citep{srivastava2025memorygraft}. MPBench
and MemSecBench follow poison from untrusted input to stored memory to action
\citep{dash2026mpbench,chen2026memsecbench}.

\paragraph{Defences.} PIGuard scores a single text and is built to avoid over-rejecting benign text
\citep{li2025piguard}. A-MemGuard validates a memory against related memories with multiple reasoning paths
\citep{wei2026amemguard}. BlindGuard learns normal interaction patterns between agents, not between memory records
\citep{miao2026blindguard}. Origin-bound methods attach authority to where a record came from and stop a record from
gaining authority through summarisation \citep{louck2026tmanm}. CPMG uses context as A-MemGuard does, at the cost of
one encoder pass as PIGuard does. Its authority policy is a much simpler form of the origin-bound idea.

\section{Threat Model}

The attacker can write content into any channel the agent ingests and can choose its wording. The attacker cannot
modify the metadata that the system records about a memory (channel, authority tier of the source, parent tool
call) and cannot modify the guard. The attacker wants a stored record to change the agent's output or tool call
later. The three levels are those of Figure~\ref{fig:levels}. \textbf{L1}: one record holds the complete malicious
instruction. \textbf{L2}: the instruction is in a different record from the one being scored and takes effect when
both are retrieved. \textbf{L3}: the instruction is conditional on a trigger string and takes effect only when a
later query contains it. A guard must also keep useful memory, so we report false alarms on matched benign inputs.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\input{figs/arch}
\caption{CPMG. The write checkpoint sees the left three segments. The retrieval checkpoint sees all of them.
Both use the same encoder architecture and the same two outputs.}
\label{fig:arch}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Input.} CPMG is a DeBERTa-v3-base cross-encoder \citep{he2023debertav3}. Figure~\ref{fig:arch} shows its
input: provenance tokens, the memory record, the interaction the record came from, the current query or planned
action, and three co-retrieved neighbours, separated by \texttt{[SEP]}, in at most 512 tokens. The source, the query
and each neighbour have budgets of 32, 48 and 64 tokens, and the record receives the rest.

\paragraph{Provenance envelope.} Each memory carries four fields recorded by the system: channel, authority tier of
the source (user, internal, external, unknown), age, and parent tool call. Each value is a special token, so
record content cannot overwrite it.

\paragraph{Two outputs.} The head has two independent sigmoid outputs. The \emph{poison} score answers: is
attacker-written content present in this context, in the record or in a neighbour? The \emph{activation} score
answers: does the current query carry the trigger that this content is conditional on? A dormant record under an
ordinary query is poison${}=1$, activation${}=0$. One risk label cannot express this state, because it would have to
call the same record harmful at write time and harmless at retrieval time.\footnote{An earlier version of our
dataset used one label and contained this conflict; see Appendix~\ref{app:v1}.}

\paragraph{Two checkpoints.} When a record is written no later query exists, so the write model sees provenance,
record and source only. At retrieval the full input is available. ``Write+retrieval'' takes the maximum of the two
poison scores.

\paragraph{Training.} The loss is the sum of two class-weighted binary cross-entropy terms. The activation term is
masked for records from sources with no known trigger. We train with AdamW, learning rate
[[run:cpmg_full:config.lr:sci]], [[run:cpmg_full:config.epochs]] epochs, effective batch size 16 and bf16 autocast, and
keep the checkpoint with the best mean validation AUROC over the two outputs. Provenance tokens are removed from
15\% of training examples so that the model can score records whose provenance it has not seen. The supervised
contrastive term from our proposal was not implemented.

\paragraph{Authority policy.} Separately from the model, a deterministic rule states that a memory whose source
authority is external or unknown can never authorise a protected action (payment, sending or sharing data,
changing settings, deletion).

\section{Data}
\label{sec:data}

\subsection{Sources}

We use public data only (Table~\ref{tab:data}): AgentPoison \citep{chen2024agentpoison}, Agent Security Bench (ASB)
\citep{zhang2025asb}, MemoryGraft \citep{srivastava2025memorygraft}, memshield-bench \citep{pow2026memshield} and
NotInject \citep{li2025piguard}. MPBench \citep{dash2026mpbench} has no data release and is not used. Two sources
needed records to be produced before use.

\emph{AgentPoison} ships clean knowledge bases and injection code but no poisoned records. We apply the authors'
injection sentence, verbatim, to their StrategyQA and EHRAgent records. Each poisoned record has a clean twin, the
same record without the injected part. \emph{ASB} ships poisoned plan memory only. We ran ASB's clean planning path
with a local Qwen3-8B model for the same agents and tasks to obtain clean plans. This gives ASB a generator
confound (poisoned plans come from gpt-4o-mini, clean plans from Qwen3-8B), discussed under Limitations. The
poisoned entries of memshield-bench were written by that dataset's author. NotInject (339 benign prompts that
contain trigger-like words) is used only to measure false alarms.

