\subsection{Matched cases}

Only AgentPoison exposes both the injected span and the trigger exactly, so L2 and L3 cases are derived from its
records. Table~\ref{tab:variants} lists the ten cases built from one poisoned record $P$, its clean twin $C$, the
injected span (the \emph{payload}), its trigger $x$ and the original question $q$.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\begin{adjustbox}{max width=\linewidth}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llllcc@{}}
\toprule
Case & Record & Neighbours & Query & Poi. & Act. \\
\midrule
L1 poisoned       & \atk{$P$} & clean & $q$ & 1 & 0 \\
L1 clean twin     & $C$ & clean & $q$ & 0 & 0 \\
L2                & $C$ & clean + \atk{payload} & $q$ & 1 & 0 \\
\midrule
L3 active         & \atk{$P$} & clean & $q$ + \atk{$x$} & 1 & 1 \\
L3 wrong trigger  & \atk{$P$} & clean & $q$ + $y$ & 1 & 0 \\
L3 overlap        & \atk{$P$} & clean & $q$ + $s$ & 1 & 0 \\
\midrule
L2+L3 active        & $C$ & clean + \atk{payload} & $q$ + \atk{$x$} & 1 & 1 \\
L2+L3 wrong trigger & $C$ & clean + \atk{payload} & $q$ + $y$ & 1 & 0 \\
L2+L3 overlap       & $C$ & clean + \atk{payload} & $q$ + $s$ & 1 & 0 \\
\midrule
Trigger, no payload & $C$ & clean & $q$ + $x$ & 0 & 0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{adjustbox}
\caption{The ten cases derived from one AgentPoison record, with their poison and activation labels. $y$ is a
different trigger; $s$ is a 3--6 word span copied from the record's own clean text. Attacker-written parts are in
orange. Within each block the record and neighbours are identical, so only the query separates the labels.}
\label{tab:variants}
\end{table}

Two kinds of negative make the activation task hard. The \emph{wrong-trigger} negative stops a model from scoring
activation by noticing an unusual string in the query. The \emph{overlap} negative stops it from scoring activation
by noticing that the query repeats text from the context, which ordinary follow-up questions also do. No input
appears twice with different labels.

\subsection{Triggers}

AgentPoison ships five trigger strings: its optimised trigger, an earlier optimised trigger left in a code
comment, the BadChain phrase, and two seed phrases used to start optimisation. Five strings are too few to learn
that activation means \emph{the query contains the string this record names}; a model trained on a few triggers
memorises them. We therefore keep the real triggers \textbf{for testing only}. For training and validation, each
AgentPoison record is injected with its own random trigger. Half are sequences of 3--8 tokens from the BERT
vocabulary, which is the form of AgentPoison's optimised triggers. Half are 3--6 word spans cut from StrategyQA
paragraphs that appear nowhere else in our data. These training triggers are synthetic. Together with the ASB clean plans above and the rewordings
of Section~\ref{sec:para}, they are the only generated content we use. Training has
[[stats:train:n_unique_triggers]] distinct triggers. In the test set, half of the AgentPoison records carry one of
the five real triggers and half carry new random triggers. No trigger string is shared between splits, and no real
trigger occurs anywhere in training or validation data ([[stats:real_triggers_in_train_or_val]] occurrences by
string search).

\subsection{Splits and provenance}

Records are grouped by source record or scenario, and a group with all of its derived cases goes to one split
([[stats:groups_crossing_splits]] groups cross splits). ASB is split by agent. All records of all ten ASB agents
also form a separate cross-source test for a model trained without ASB. Neighbours are the three most similar
clean records from the same source and split.

No source records per-item provenance, so we assign provenance from each source's documented origin; for example,
AgentPoison's StrategyQA knowledge base is tagged web/external. Provenance is therefore constant within a source. A
classifier that sees only provenance scores [[prov_only:P_src_AgentPoison:auroc]] AUROC within AgentPoison, so
provenance does not leak the label. For the same reason it cannot carry any signal beyond dataset identity; we
return to this in Section~\ref{sec:prov}.

\section{Experimental Setup}

\paragraph{Baselines.} (i) \emph{Content-only DeBERTa}: the same model and training, with the record as its only
input. (ii) \emph{PIGuard}, public checkpoint, zero-shot on the record \citep{li2025piguard}. (iii) A \emph{keyword
filter} with 18 injection patterns. (iv) \emph{TF-IDF with logistic regression}, on the record alone and on the full
flattened CPMG input. (v) A \emph{copy rule} with no training: the share of the query's extra tokens that also
occur as one contiguous run in the record or its neighbours. (vi) A \emph{quoted-copy rule}$^\dagger$: the same, but
counting only strings that the context puts in quotation marks before the words ``in the question''. This rule
encodes the wording of AgentPoison's two injection sentences. It shows what knowledge of the attack template
achieves without any learning.

\paragraph{Ablations.} CPMG without provenance, without the query, without neighbours, and the write-time model
alone.

\paragraph{Metrics.} AUROC and true-positive rate at 1\% false-positive rate (TPR@1\%), on the matched positives
and negatives of each case. Thresholds are chosen on validation data (maximum F1) and applied unchanged to test
data. CPMG, content-only and CPMG without provenance are trained with [[seeds:cpmg_full]] seeds; we report the
mean, and the standard deviation where it is not zero at the printed precision. Other models use one seed. The
dataset is built once. Everything runs on one laptop GPU (RTX 5060, 8\,GB).

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Who can detect what}
\label{sec:main}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/capability_matrix.pdf}
\caption{Test AUROC of every model on the four tasks. Activation columns use the five real triggers, against
wrong-trigger and overlap negatives together. White cells are at chance. $^\dagger$The quoted-copy rule encodes the
attack template. Exact values with deviations are in Table~\ref{tab:main}.}
\label{fig:matrix}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:matrix} summarises the main result; Table~\ref{tab:main} in the appendix has exact values.

\paragraph{L1 does not separate methods.} Every trained model detects direct attacks almost perfectly, including
TF-IDF on the record alone ([[tfidf_memory:P_L1:auroc]] AUROC). The injected instructions in public datasets are
lexically distinctive. We base no claim on L1. The one visible difference is at low false-positive rates, where
zero-shot PIGuard detects [[piguard:P_L1:tpr_at_1fpr:%]]\% of L1 records at 1\% FPR and CPMG
[[cpmg_full:P_L1:tpr_at_1fpr:%]]\%. On memshield-bench, the smallest and hardest source, CPMG at retrieval
([[cpmg_full:P_src_memshield-bench:auroc]]) is not better than the content-only model
([[content_only:P_src_memshield-bench:auroc]]); see Table~\ref{tab:persource}.

\paragraph{L2 needs the neighbours, and nothing more.} The scored record is identical for L2 positives and
negatives, so every model that reads only the record is at chance (content-only:
[[content_only:P_L2:auroc:mean]]; PIGuard: [[piguard:P_L2:auroc]]). CPMG reaches [[cpmg_full:P_L2:auroc:mean]]. So
does TF-IDF over the full input ([[tfidf_full:P_L2:auroc]]): once the payload is visible among the neighbours it is
as easy to detect as in L1. L2 built from public data therefore measures whether a guard \emph{looks at}
co-retrieved records. It does not measure reasoning over fragments that are each benign, and we make no such
claim.

\paragraph{L3 needs the query, and a relation between query and context.} All positives and negatives in the
activation columns share the record and neighbours and differ only in the query. Models without the query are at
chance. TF-IDF over the full input is also at chance ([[tfidf_full:A_L3_hard_real:auroc]]), because the test
triggers are unseen strings. The copy rule separates the right trigger from a wrong trigger but not from overlap
([[lexmatch:A_L3_ovl_real:auroc]]), because it cannot tell a repeated trigger from repeated harmless text; its
combined score is [[lexmatch:A_L3_hard_real:auroc]].

CPMG reaches [[cpmg_full:A_L3_hard_real:auroc:mean]] AUROC (seed-42 bootstrap 95\% CI
[[ci:cpmg_full:A_L3_hard_real]]) and [[cpmg_full:A_L3_hard_real:tpr_at_1fpr:mean%]]\% TPR at 1\% FPR on the five
real triggers, having been trained only on random ones (per-trigger values in Table~\ref{tab:pertrigger}). When
the payload is in a neighbour (L2+L3) it reaches [[cpmg_full:A_L23_hard_real:auroc:mean]]. When the query carries a
trigger and the context holds no payload, it raises the activation flag in
[[cpmg_full:A_trig_only:fpr:mean%]]\% of cases.

\paragraph{Each input segment supports one capability.} The ablation rows of Figure~\ref{fig:matrix} show a clean
pattern. Without the query, activation falls to chance ([[abl_no_query:A_L3_hard_real:auroc]]) and poison
detection is unchanged. Without neighbours, L2 falls to chance ([[abl_no_nbrs:P_L2:auroc]]) and L2+L3 activation
falls to [[abl_no_nbrs:A_L23_hard_real:auroc]], while L3 activation, where the payload is in the record itself,
is unaffected. The write-time model behaves like the content-only baseline on every context-dependent case. This is
the cost of scoring before the query exists and the reason for the second checkpoint. Removing provenance changes
nothing (Section~\ref{sec:prov}).

\subsection{Rewording the attack separates the model from a rule}
\label{sec:para}

Figure~\ref{fig:matrix} has an uncomfortable last row: the quoted-copy rule scores
[[lexquote:A_L3_hard_real:auroc]]. AgentPoison's injection sentence always names its trigger in quotation marks
before the words ``in the question'', and a regular expression that knows this recovers the trigger exactly. On the
attacks as published, a learned model has no accuracy advantage over a rule written with knowledge of the template.

An attacker is not bound to the published wording. We therefore rewrote the injected sentence of every
AgentPoison test record with an LLM (Qwen3-8B), leaving the question, the trigger, the neighbours and the labels
unchanged, and re-scored all models with no retraining. The LLM saw a placeholder in place of the trigger, so it
could not alter the trigger string. A rewrite was accepted only if it shared at most 60\% of its word set with the
original. We use two conditions. \emph{Reworded}: same meaning, different words
([[para:para:unique]] distinct sentences over [[para:para:accepted]] records). \emph{Reworded, no quotes}: in
addition the trigger is not quoted and the words ``in the question'' are not used
([[para:paranq:unique]] distinct sentences). For example, one injected sentence with the BadChain trigger
becomes: ``[[para_example:paranq]]''

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/generalisation.pdf}
\caption{Activation AUROC (L3, against wrong-trigger and overlap negatives) as the test moves away from the
training data. The dashed line is chance. CPMG is never retrained. $^\dagger$Encodes the original attack
template.}
\label{fig:gen}
\end{figure}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4.5pt}
\begin{adjustbox}{max width=\linewidth}
[[table:robust]]
\end{adjustbox}
\caption{Original attack (orig.), reworded attack (rew.) and reworded attack without quotation marks around the
trigger (rew.-nq). Poison: AgentPoison L1 records against their clean twins. Activation: L3 against wrong-trigger
and overlap negatives, all test triggers. No model is retrained. TPR in percent. $^\dagger$Encodes the original
attack template.}
\label{tab:robust}
\end{table*}

Figure~\ref{fig:gen} and Table~\ref{tab:robust} show the outcome. Under plain rewording the quoted-copy rule still
works ([[lexquote:A_L3_hard@para:auroc]]), because [[para:para:rule_match]] of [[para:para:accepted]] rewrites keep
the quoted trigger. Once the quoting is gone the rule is at chance ([[lexquote:A_L3_hard@paranq:auroc]]). CPMG
stays at [[cpmg_full:A_L3_hard@paranq:auroc]] AUROC and [[cpmg_full:A_L3_hard@paranq:tpr_at_1fpr:%]]\% TPR at 1\%
FPR. Rewording also weakens lexical poison detection: TF-IDF over the full input drops from
[[tfidf_full:P_L1_ap:tpr_at_1fpr:mean%]]\% to [[tfidf_full:P_L1_ap@paranq:tpr_at_1fpr:mean%]]\% TPR at 1\% FPR on
L1, while CPMG stays at [[cpmg_full:P_L1_ap@paranq:tpr_at_1fpr:mean%]]\%.

This is the evidence that CPMG learned something more general than the template: it identifies the string that the
poisoned content makes its condition, however that condition is phrased, and checks whether the query contains it.
The test has limits. The rewrites come from one model, stay close to the original meaning, and the attacker did
not optimise against CPMG itself.

\subsection{An unseen record format misleads the model}
\label{sec:xtemplate}

To test transfer to an attack the model has not seen, we trained CPMG without any EHRAgent record and tested it on
EHRAgent. That model has seen only StrategyQA's injected records. EHRAgent uses a different sentence (``Because
there is \ldots\ in the question, this means that we have to delete the data''), a different harmful action
(a database read replaced by a delete) and a different record layout.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering\small
\begin{adjustbox}{max width=\linewidth}
[[table:xtemplate]]
\end{adjustbox}
\caption{EHRAgent test records, AUROC ([[n:A_L3_hard_ehr:n_pos]] record groups). P = poison, A = activation
against wrong-trigger and overlap negatives. ``Trained w/o EHRAgent'' never saw EHRAgent's injection sentence or
record format. $^\dagger$Encodes both injection sentences.}
\label{tab:xtemplate}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:xtemplate} shows a split result. Poison detection is unaffected
([[cpmg_noehr:P_L1_ehr:auroc]] on L1 and [[cpmg_noehr:P_L2_ehr:auroc]] on L2), but L1 is a weak test here: an
injected EHRAgent record contains a database delete, and the content-only model also scores
[[content_only:P_L1_ehr:auroc:mean]] on it. Activation works when the payload is in a neighbour
([[cpmg_noehr:A_L23_hard_ehr:auroc]]). We do not read this as transfer to new wording, because the two injection
sentences share the phrase ``there is \ldots\ in the question''; the rewording test of Section~\ref{sec:para} is
our evidence on wording. Activation fails completely when the payload is in the record
([[cpmg_noehr:A_L3_hard_ehr:auroc]]): the model outputs activation${}=1$ for every such record, whatever the query.
The cause is the record format. A poisoned EHRAgent record stores its own triggered question as its first line, so
the trigger already appears twice inside the record. StrategyQA records never do this, and a model trained only on
them treats the stored copy as if it were the query. A model trained with EHRAgent records does not make this
mistake ([[cpmg_full:A_L3_hard_ehr:auroc:mean]]). The sample is small ([[n:A_L3_hard_ehr:n_pos]] groups), but the
failure is total, and it shows that the model's notion of ``where the trigger is'' depends on record layouts it has
seen.

\subsection{Provenance: no effect, and no way to test it}
\label{sec:prov}

Our proposal expected system-recorded provenance to help the guard. We found no evidence for this, for two
separate reasons.

\emph{The model does not use the tokens.} Training without provenance gives the same results on every task
(Figure~\ref{fig:matrix}). Swapping each test record's provenance for that of a different source changes the
poison score by [[cpmg_full:provswap_delta:mean_abs_delta:mean]] on average and flips
[[cpmg_full:provswap_delta:decision_flip_rate:mean%]]\% of decisions (Table~\ref{tab:provswap}).

\emph{The data could not have shown a benefit.} In every public source we found, provenance is a property of the
dataset and not of the record: all records of a source, clean and poisoned, arrive through one channel with one
authority tier. A provenance feature can then only identify the dataset. Testing the provenance hypothesis needs a
benchmark in which the same content appears with different origins and the correct decision depends on the origin,
for example an instruction that is legitimate when it comes from the user and an attack when it comes from a web
page. We consider this the most useful missing resource for this line of work.

The authority policy suffers from the same problem. On real L1 test records it blocks
[[policy:test:label=1|blocked]] of the [[policy:test:label=1|protected_action]] poisoned records that mention a
protected action and [[policy:test:label=0|blocked]] of the [[policy:test:label=0|protected_action]] clean records that mention one,
and it blocks no ASB record because ASB's injections arrive through the user prompt. With
provenance fixed per source the policy is a per-source switch, and this evaluation says little about its value.

\subsection{False alarms, transfer across sources, and cost}
\label{sec:false}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering\small
\begin{adjustbox}{max width=\linewidth}
[[table:falsealarm]]
\end{adjustbox}
\caption{False alarms of the poison output at the validation threshold, in percent. NotInject: benign prompts with
trigger-like words. Trigger, no payload: clean record and neighbours with a trigger in the query.}
\label{tab:false}
\end{table}

\paragraph{False alarms.} On NotInject, CPMG flags [[cpmg_full:notinject:fpr:%]]\% of benign prompts
(Table~\ref{tab:false}). NotInject is far from our training data: its inputs are user prompts with no query or
neighbours, and its provenance tags never occur in training. The rate is still too high for deployment and it
varies across seeds, which shows that the poison output over-reacts to instruction-like wording outside the sources
it was trained on. PIGuard was built against this failure; the [[piguard:notinject:fpr:%]]\% we report for it is
at the threshold our validation data selects, which is not PIGuard's intended operating point.

\paragraph{Unseen source.} A CPMG model trained without ASB ranks all ASB records correctly
([[cpmg_noasb:asb_xsource:auroc]] AUROC) but its validation threshold transfers badly: it flags only
[[cpmg_noasb:asb_xsource:recall:%]]\% of poisoned ASB records (Table~\ref{tab:xsource}). A threshold chosen on
one source is not safe on another. ASB is also an easy source, since a keyword filter reaches
[[keyword:asb_xsource:auroc]] AUROC on it.

\paragraph{Cost.} CPMG takes [[run:cpmg_full:latency_ms_bs1:.1f]]\,ms per check on a laptop GPU, the same as the
content-only model ([[run:content_only:latency_ms_bs1:.1f]]\,ms; Table~\ref{tab:cost}). Reading the context costs
nothing in model size and almost nothing in latency at this input length. We did not run A-MemGuard, so we cannot
report the cost ratio to an LLM-based contextual defence that our proposal set out to measure.

\section{Conclusion}

A guard that scores memory records one at a time cannot see an attack that depends on a neighbouring record or on
a later query. This holds for a strong pretrained filter as much as for a keyword list, because the information is
not in its input. Giving a small cross-encoder the query and the co-retrieved records, and asking it two
questions instead of one, removes that blindness at no extra cost per check. The model generalises to real
triggers it never saw and to attacks reworded by an LLM, where a rule built from the attack template fails. It does
not generalise to a record layout it has not seen, it raises too many false alarms on out-of-distribution benign
text, and it makes no use of provenance. Public benchmarks do not yet contain what is needed to test the last
point: the same content arriving from different origins.

\section*{Limitations}

\textbf{Template-based attacks.} All L2 and L3 cases derive from AgentPoison, which uses one injection sentence per
agent. Scores at ceiling reflect how regular these attacks are. Our rewording test uses one LLM and does not
optimise against CPMG.
\textbf{Synthetic training triggers.} Training triggers are random strings inserted with the authors' template.
Test triggers are real, but there are only five.
\textbf{Derived cases.} L2, L3 and L2+L3 are transformations of real records, not attacks observed in a running
agent, and L2 does not test composition of fragments that are each benign.
\textbf{Offline evaluation.} We measure detection, not end-to-end attack success in an agent, and not the effect on
useful recall (for example on LongMemEval). Both were in our proposal and are not done.
\textbf{Missing baselines.} A-MemGuard and an adapted BlindGuard were planned and not run.
\textbf{ASB confound.} Clean and poisoned ASB plans come from different generators, so ASB results say little about
content.
\textbf{Provenance.} Assigned per source from documentation, and therefore untestable here.
\textbf{Seeds.} Three seeds for the main models, one for the rest; the dataset is built once.

\section*{Ethics Statement}

This work studies defences. All attack data come from public research datasets, and the records we produced apply
the original authors' published injection templates to their published data. The reworded attacks are a robustness
test of existing published attacks and add no new capability. We release code and derived data so that the
evaluation can be reproduced.

